This directory contains sample configuration files used for roadwarrior
remote access using hybrid authentication. In this setup, the VPN
gateway authenticates to the client using a certificate, and the client
authenticates to the VPN gateway using a login and a password.

Moreover, this setup makes use of ISAKMP mode config to autoconfigure
the client. After a successful login, the client will receive an
internal address, netmask and DNS from the VPN gateway.

Once installed (via `make install`, or by your distribution's package),
this directory's contents live under the roadwarrior/ subdirectory of
this project's configuration templates directory -- a location of its
own, not a subdirectory of /usr/share/doc, specifically so it survives
on distributions that strip /usr/share/doc/* on minimal/container
installs. See racoon.conf.default's own header comment for how to
locate that path on your system.


Server setup
============
The server setup needs racoon built with the following options:
configure --enable-natt --enable-frag --enable-hybrid --enable-dpd \
	  --sysconfdir=/etc/racoon

server/racoon.conf is a VPN gateway using authentication against the
system password database (auth_source system) by default, with a
locally configured pool of addresses; see the auth_source comment in
that file for PAM- and LDAP-backed alternatives (PAM is recommended for
production use -- it allows SSSD/LDAP/Kerberos/2FA integration without
further racoon.conf changes). RADIUS-backed authentication is not
covered by a dedicated example here; racoon.conf(5) documents the
"radius" auth_source and the "radiuscfg" block it requires (built with
--with-libradius).

This configuration can be used with the Cisco VPN client if it is set
up to use hybrid authentication (aka mutual group authentication,
available in Cisco VPN client version 4.0.5 and above). The group
password configured in the Cisco VPN client is not used by racoon.

After you have installed /etc/racoon/racoon.conf, you also need to
install a server certificate and key at /etc/racoon/certs/server.crt
and /etc/racoon/certs/server.key (paths matching this example's own
"path certificate" directive).


Client setup
============
The client setup needs racoon built with the following options:
configure --enable-natt --enable-frag --enable-hybrid --enable-dpd \
	  --enable-adminport --sysconfdir=/etc/racoon --localstatedir=/var

Copy client/racoon.conf to /etc/racoon/racoon.conf, and copy your
client certificate and key to /etc/racoon/certs/client.crt and
/etc/racoon/certs/client.key, plus the certificate authority that
signed the VPN gateway's certificate to /etc/racoon/certs/ca.crt
(paths matching this example's own "path certificate" directive and
its commented-out "remote" block).

NOTE: client/phase1-up.sh and client/phase1-down.sh here are minimal,
historical example hooks (they only log the Mode Config environment
and stop). They are installed by `make install` as reference material
alongside this README, but are not wired into any active racoon.conf
and are not the functional hooks used by the example above. For a
real, maintained split-DNS and routing hook set (DNS backend detection
across systemd-resolved/NetworkManager/resolvconf/dnsmasq, route/SPD
management, whitelist input validation, and a full test suite), use
src/racoon/scripts/ instead, installed unconditionally to
/etc/racoon/scripts/ as a real package component -- this is what
client/racoon.conf's own "script" directives actually point at. See
doc/admin/split-dns.html for complete documentation.

Once this is done, you can run racoon, and then you can start
the VPN using racoonctl:
racoonctl vc -u username vpn-gateway.example.net

Where username is your login, and vpn-gateway.example.net is
the DNS or IP address of the VPN gateway. racoonctl will prompt
you for the password.

The password can be stored in the psk.txt file instead of being
prompted for interactively. In that situation, add this directive to
the remote section of racoon.conf:
	 xauth_login "username";
where username is your login, and add a matching "username <password>"
entry to psk.txt -- see examples/psk.txt.example for the exact format.

Note that for now there is no feedback in racoonctl if the authentication
fails. Peek at the racoon logs to discover what goes wrong.

In order to disconnect from the VPN, do this:
racoonctl vd vpn-gateway.example.net

This configuration should be compatible with the Cisco VPN 3000 using
hybrid authentication, though this has not been tested.
